.SH NAME
strfile, unstr \- "create a random access file for storing strings"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B strfile
[\fB\-iorsx\fP]
[\fB\-c\fP \fIchar\fP]
\fIsource_file\fP
[\fIoutput_file\fP]
.br
.B unstr
\fIsource_file\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B strfile
reads a file containing groups of lines separated by a line containing
a single percent
.'"' mdoc: .Ql \&%
\&%
sign and creates a data file which contains
a header structure and a table of file offsets for each group of lines.
This allows random access of the strings.
.PP
The output file, if not specified on the command line, is named
\fIsource_file Ns Sy .out .\fP
.PP
The options are as follows:
.TP
\fB\-c\fP \fIchar\fP
Change the delimiting character from the percent sign to
\fIchar .\fP
.TP
\fB\-i\fP
Ignore case when ordering the strings.
.TP
\fB\-o\fP
Order the strings in alphabetical order.
The offset table will be sorted in the alphabetical order of the
groups of lines referenced.
Any initial non-alphanumeric characters are ignored.
This option causes the
.'"' mdoc: .Dv STR_ORDERED
STR_ORDERED
bit in the header
\fIstr_flags\fP
field to be set.
.TP
\fB\-r\fP
Randomize access to the strings.
Entries in the offset table will be randomly ordered.
This option causes the
.'"' mdoc: .Dv STR_RANDOM
STR_RANDOM
bit in the header
\fIstr_flags\fP
field to be set.
.TP
\fB\-s\fP
Run silently; don't give a summary message when finished.
.TP
\fB\-x\fP
Note that each alphabetic character in the groups of lines is rotated
13 positions in a simple caesar cipher.
This option causes the
.'"' mdoc: .Dv STR_ROTATED
STR_ROTATED
bit in the header
\fIstr_flags\fP
field to be set.
.PP
.PP
The format of the header is:
.nf
#define	VERSION	1
unsigned long	str_version;	/* version number */
unsigned long	str_numstr;	/* # of strings in the file */
unsigned long	str_longlen;	/* length of longest string */
unsigned long	str_shortlen;	/* length of shortest string */
#define	STR_RANDOM	0x1	/* randomized pointers */
#define	STR_ORDERED	0x2	/* ordered pointers */
#define	STR_ROTATED	0x4	/* rot-13'd text */
unsigned long	str_flags;	/* bit field for flags */
char str_delim;			/* delimiting character */
.fi
.PP
All fields are written in big-endian byte order.
.PP
The purpose of
.B u
is to undo the work of
.B strfile
It prints out the strings contained in the file
\fIsource_file\fP
in the order that they are listed in the header file
\fIsource_file Ns Sy .dat\fP
to standard output.
It is possible to create sorted versions of input files by using
\fB\-o\fP
when
.B s
is run and then using
.B u
to dump them out in the table order.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fIstrfile.out\fP
default output file.
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
byteorder(3) ,
fortune(6)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B s
utility first appeared in
.'"' mdoc: .Bx 4.4 .
4.4 .

